From de3b463a0adbea02a5bfe3f319fffb665229e543 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: agent-keys <noreply@agent-keys.invalid>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 12:00:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/20] fix(operator): signer-migrate checks every Safe before
 the first changes hands

With the fleet identity Safe as well as the service Safe, each Safe was checked just before its
own handover: a refusal on the second came after the first had been handed over, and the
operator was left half migrated. A point of #250 in the agent-keys repository (B1).

- signer/migrate.ts: the reads and refusals of one Safe's migration are one function,
  `preflight`: the Safe's owners and threshold, its modules, guard and fallback handler, the
  owner's grant, the Kernel's mandate, the agent proof where there is no ERC-8004 identity and
  the agent NFT's owner where there is one (moved out of the NFT's step). It sends nothing.
  `migrateTargets` runs it for every target before the first handover, then each Safe's
  migration, which runs it again right before its own handover. A refusal names the Safe:
  "the fleet identity Safe <address>, read before any Safe changes hands: ...". It returns the
  command's output (`MigrationOutput`).
- cli/commands/signer-migrate.ts: the verb runs migrateTargets over migrationTargets; the help
  says that both Safes are checked before either changes hands.
- tests: unit, with a second Safe in the mocked chain: a guard, a fallback handler, the agent
  EOA as a module, threshold 2 or other owners on the fleet identity Safe are refused while the
  service Safe is still the EOA's and nothing is sent; so is the fleet identity Safe's missing
  agent proof when the service Safe has an identity (no NFT moves, the Kernel signs nothing), and
  an agent NFT that is neither the EOA's nor the Kernel's; with both clear, each is handed over
  in turn and the output lists both. In the full loop, on the snapshot: a run over both Safes
  refuses at the fleet identity Safe, which the mandate does not admit, and the service Safe and
  its NFT are still the EOA's. Each rule was broken once on purpose and a test caught it (no
  reading of every Safe first, no NFT owner check).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
